what is dark souls if not death a series  notorious for difficulty and a narrative  packed to the brim with grave themes  that will have some players looking at  the you died screen more than they  actually play the game  if you've never played the soulsborne  series look no further than chammi's  this is what dark souls 3 is like for a  quick familiarization  though in the endless comparisons of  other games to the series solely when  they include any semblance of difficulty  it's easy to be left with a reductive  view of the series and its use of death  a kind of hard for hardness sake  but the difficulty failure and focus on  death which one finds in a fromsoft game  is more than this it is a constitutive  piece of what makes a video game a work  of art failure in games helps to  demarcate them as an art form a form  filled with interactivity and engagement  unlike any other and even more so death  in the soulsborne is not just a gameplay  mechanic with the narrative introduction  of the hollow and the process of  halloween death takes on an essential  story element both in giving an in-game  explanation for the mechanic of  respawning and going beyond the game to  tell a story about us as players even  casting judgment on us deeming us hollow  if we fail to meet its mark this going  beyond at the confluence of gameplay and  narrative is where the soulsborne series  enters the ludo narrative and in doing  so shows off a unique aesthetic  experience only found in the video game  i want to explore all of this from  gameplay to story to player and all the  artistic in between as they are richly  presented to us in the concepts of death  and halloween in dark souls  plus it's halloween  as i look at death failure and halloween  in the soulsborne franchise i want to  make use of a really insightful piece of  writing a philosophy phd dissertation  from one zach jurgensen who wrote about  the artistic merit of games in his paper  titled the aesthetic value of video  games an analysis of interactivity  gameplay and player performance this  piece highlights a lot of unique and  important aspects of what makes games so  artistically meaningful a key element of  which being the fact that they are games  one of the ways that jurgensen details  that video games can provide unique  merit is how they can employ failure  firstly a game that is too easy is  simply not engaging jurgensen notes that  if a player is guaranteed success  without the threat of failure then  engagement with the game turns into  manipulation as opposed to interaction  he goes on to cite research which finds  players enjoying games more when they  occasionally fail as opposed to when  failure is completely absent for it to  be a game there has to be some chance  you lose if there isn't then the  developer risks the player becoming  disengaged and according to jurgensen  the potential for a unique aesthetic  experience found through interactivity  and immersion is lost  and in his next statement much to the  glee of die hard no easy mode allowed  souls fans jurgensen concludes games  that are too easy or require little  attention to rules of skill fail to take  full advantage of what sets video games  apart from other artistic mediums the  potential for a highly immersive  interactive experience easy games negate  the value of working within the video  game medium  now even with these statements there is  plenty of room for people to argue about  whether or not soulsborne games still  need to be as hard as they are or if you  like to be special that they actually  aren't that hard at all and definitely  need to be harder i'm not here to weigh  in too much on the details either way  but i do want to say that the presence  of this difficulty this guarantee of  failure is to a large degree absolutely  necessary  it becomes a vital component of the  experience of the game failure is one of  the variables that interactivity and  engagement are most dependent on to  really experience what a video game is  this engagement and thus this failure  must be there  and it must be there in the right manner  pulling from another writer jesper  jewell jurgensen spends half of his  discussion on failure describing how it  has to be the right kind of failure  failure caused by user action  unsurprisingly we find a good example of  this right kind in dark souls a series  notorious save for a few notable  exceptions for being fair with its  deaths and causes of failure if you die  in dark souls most of the time it's on  you whether that be for rushing in or  not taking the time to note irrelevant  detail while they certainly don't have a  perfect track record fromsoft games  generally embody this right kind of  failure pretty well keeping the player  engaged by having their failure  determined by their own actions when  jurgensen says that easy games negate  the value of working within the medium  he is saying that ease completely fails  to utilize one of the core aspects that  demarcate games from other media easy  games can of course still exist not  every game has to flaunt how different  it is from movies and books and the like  plus there are plenty of relatively  chill games that might be thought of as  easy but still have a decent amount of  failure stardew valley comes to mind  however in discussions of the medium of  games and how it can grow and develop  and deliver the artistic experiences  that help make life worth living the  presence and mechanic of failure must be  included  but as i said death in dark souls is not  just a gameplay mechanic it is also a  prominent narrative theme permeating the  game world and especially the differing  game endings and importantly it's the  path to becoming hollow hollowness in  dark souls is essentially the  destruction of the mind in the in-game  story a curse has afflicted the land  preventing those who contract it from  dying most of these undead eventually  lose their minds and become hostile  zombie-like figures they have gone  hollow  your character in dark souls 1 the  chosen undead is one of these undead who  is to some degree hollow narratively  this explains and justifies why the  player character can respond after death  instead of just accepting the logical  inconsistency of dying and re-trying to  fight two seconds later i say two  seconds as if fromsoft would ever give  us a boss run back that isn't at least a  minute long the game justifies why the  failure of death is not permanent the  player character is undead so if they  fail a fight and die they just come back  to life to fight again and while this  exact kind of halloween isn't nominally  present in every game of the series at  least in the same ways there is  generally a similar ludonarrative theme  throughout  in the original dark souls after an  in-game death the player character gains  the appearance of a hollow a living set  of skin and bones and this halloween  also comes with some gameplay  restrictions but if the player continues  to play the game the player character  does not quite go hollow per se  throughout the game and the rest of the  series we realize that going hollow  happens chiefly with the loss of purpose  direction or meaning crestfallen  characters that lose hope and give up on  living admittedly undead living but  living nonetheless go hollow but the  player character doesn't as long as the  player keeps playing  and as the player keeps playing and  dying along comes another more meta  experience characteristic of dark souls  getting fed up with the game and its  deaths and giving up leaving the game  before you complete or beat its  narrative journey i know this experience  well as it was me when i came across the  first real boss of the game i must have  given up on dark souls 1 between 3 and 8  times when trying to best the taurus  demon the first boss after the tutorial  while there were some issues that i  would classify now as the bad kind of  failure referenced earlier failure that  did not have to do with my actions per  se or aspects of failure that did not  feel like they fit the crime boston  backs i died a lot and i wanted to give  up  and i did many times but i came back  each time sometimes after months and  eventually found out how to cheese the  boss and got through it in terminology  familiar to the game and the community  of fans i almost went hollow not just my  in-game avatar or character but me the  player when other players leave messages  in the game world or non-player  characters speak to your character and  they say not to give up hope and not to  go hollow they may be directed first to  the in-game character but as that  character's controller they then address  you the player when a player gives up on  the game without fighting to the end and  choosing that final fate to link the  flame or let it go out or something  subsequent in the later games they are  leaving their character as one of the  mindless drones to sit in the universe  of the game forever aimless and without  purpose  hollow  in this connection of gameplay to story  and metagame play to story two core  elements of the video game are being  fused this is the ludo narrative a term  made popular by the concept of  ludonarrative dissonance but a more  broad phenomenon that describes the  interplay of gameplay as ludus is the  latin word for game and narrative within  video games jurgensen discusses this  concept of ludonarrative albeit in  different terms as another core aspect  of video game aesthetics while jurgensen  uses the example of braid i think his  discussion is just as applicable to dark  souls he says with a bit of my own  subject interjection to make the quote  work by connecting the mechanics of  death failure and respawning with the  story element of halloween dark souls  successfully connects its game hood with  the narrative taking full advantage of  the interactivity video games offer  doing so creates a cohesive object of  aesthetic appreciation where the  artistic value of the narrative and  gamehood are intimately intertwined  to me this connection of gamehood and  narrative to create a unique aesthetic  experience is one of if not the core  artistic merits of video games it is a  blend of aesthetic in a medium specific  way no other kind of art could deliver  an experience of the same kind  as such to see it happen in dark souls  especially in tandem with another vital  gaming experience that of failure is to  witness and recognize a profound moment  of artistic depth  with so much tied up into a single  mechanic so much player experience and  narrative weight which is able to reach  out from the game and comment on even  judge the player themself it is clear  that a deeply artistic and aesthetically  worthwhile experience resides here at  the confluence of death and halloween in  dark souls  i really wanted to get this video out on  halloween for the title pun and i also  really want to discuss the aspect of  ludo narrative and the philosophy of  games more both how video games achieve  unique artistic status and how they can  relay traditional philosophical  knowledge hopefully this is a good  primer that you my audience will be  receptive to as it has been dark souls  centric i'm hoping to do a follow-up  video that focuses on the ludo narrative  more centrally and explores these  concepts more explicitly  well those are my closing remarks and i  hope you enjoyed the video be sure to  stick around if you want more of this  kind of content thanks
 

